add('specs/WORKFLOW_STATE_MACHINE.tla', r'''
---------------- MODULE AtlasWorkflow ----------------
EXTENDS Naturals, TLC

CONSTANTS Read, Draft, External, Restricted,
          Open, ApprovalRequired, Approved, Rejected, Completed

VARIABLES actionClass, status, approvalGranted, effectExecuted
vars == <<actionClass, status, approvalGranted, effectExecuted>>

Init ==
    /\ actionClass \in {Read, Draft, External, Restricted}
    /\ status = IF actionClass = External THEN ApprovalRequired ELSE Open
    /\ approvalGranted = FALSE
    /\ effectExecuted = FALSE

Approve ==
    /\ actionClass = External
    /\ status = ApprovalRequired
    /\ approvalGranted' = TRUE
    /\ status' = Approved
    /\ UNCHANGED <<actionClass, effectExecuted>>

Reject ==
    /\ actionClass = External
    /\ status = ApprovalRequired
    /\ status' = Rejected
    /\ approvalGranted' = FALSE
    /\ UNCHANGED <<actionClass, effectExecuted>>

Complete ==
    /\ status \in {Open, Approved}
    /\ (actionClass # External \/ approvalGranted)
    /\ actionClass # Restricted
    /\ status' = Completed
    /\ effectExecuted' = TRUE
    /\ UNCHANGED <<actionClass, approvalGranted>>

Next == Approve \/ Reject \/ Complete

SafetyNoUnapprovedExternalEffect ==
    actionClass = External /\ effectExecuted => approvalGranted

SafetyRestrictedNeverExecutes ==
    actionClass = Restricted => ~effectExecuted

Spec == Init /\ [][Next]_vars
====================================================
''')

add('specs/SECURITY_THREAT_MODEL.md', r'''
# ATLAS Security Threat Model (initial)

## Assets
- User identity and credentials
- Workspace records and uploaded documents
- Connector tokens and API secrets
- Workflow instructions and tool permissions
- AI prompts, outputs and retrieved context
- Audit events and billing/usage records

## Trust boundaries
1. Browser to control plane
2. Control plane to database/event ledger
3. Control plane to intelligence provider
4. Agent runtime to connector/tool execution
5. Tenant/workspace to tenant/workspace
6. User-approved actions to external systems

## Threats and baseline mitigations
| Threat | Baseline mitigation | Required verification |
|---|---|---|
| Cross-tenant data access | Tenant-scoped queries, RLS, isolation tests | Negative access tests for every data API |
| Prompt injection via documents/web content | Treat retrieved content as untrusted data; tool allowlists | Adversarial prompt-injection suite |
| Credential theft | Secret manager, short-lived tokens, least privilege | Secret scanning and rotation drill |
| Unsafe agent action | Capability scopes, approval gateway, action allowlist | Policy conformance tests |
| Replay/duplicate execution | Idempotency keys and deduplication | Duplicate request tests |
| Audit tampering | Hash-chained append-only records and protected backups | Tamper detection and restore test |
| Model hallucination | Source-grounded outputs, abstention and evaluation | Labelled evaluation suite |
| Denial of service | Body limits, rate limits, timeouts and quotas | Load and abuse tests |
| Supply-chain compromise | Lockfiles, dependency scans, signed builds where feasible | CI security checks |
| Sensitive data leakage | Data minimisation, redacted logs, retention controls | Log inspection and privacy tests |

## Security review requirements
Use OWASP ASVS 5.0.0 as a web application verification baseline and adapt requirements to the product. This is not a certification claim. Add a formal privacy assessment and qualified legal/security review before handling sensitive or regulated data.
''')

add('specs/ENGINEERING_STANDARDS.md', r'''
# ATLAS Engineering Standards

## Architecture
- Explicit bounded contexts and ownership.
- Modular monolith until service extraction is justified by independent scaling, deployment, security isolation or ownership.
- API contracts versioned and tested.
- Event schemas versioned; additive evolution preferred.
- Timeouts, retries with jitter and idempotency for remote calls.
- No distributed transaction assumption; use outbox/saga patterns only when the workflow requires them.

## Code quality
- Strict typing where supported.
- Formatting, linting, static analysis and dependency checks in CI.
- Small cohesive modules; no hidden global state.
- Explicit error contracts; no swallowed exceptions.
- Deterministic tests for policy and domain logic.
- Property-based and fuzz testing for parsers and state transitions when supported.

## Reliability
- Define service-level indicators before service-level objectives.
- Measure success rate, tail latency, error rate, queue age and cost per successful workflow.
- Backups and restoration tests before production use.
- Runbooks for incidents, degraded mode and provider outage.

## AI engineering
- Version models, prompts, tool schemas and evaluation datasets.
- Separate model output from validated domain state.
- Never let model text directly authorise a tool invocation.
- Enforce schemas, permission checks and policy in deterministic code.
- Record model/provider, latency, token/cost estimates and outcome.
- Evaluate task completion, factuality, calibration, refusal behaviour and injection resilience.

## Security engineering
- Deny by default.
- Least privilege, short-lived credentials and tenant isolation.
- Separate development, staging and production credentials.
- No secrets in source, logs or client bundles.
- Review permissions for every connector and action.
- Human approval for consequential side effects.

## Release gates
- Reproducible build.
- Critical test suite passes.
- No unresolved critical/high security defect.
- Data migration and rollback plan reviewed.
- Monitoring and incident owner assigned.
- User documentation and known limitations published.
''')

