# Add an architecture decision record
(root/'docs/ADR-0001-polyglot-modular-monolith.md').write_text('''# ADR-0001: Polyglot modular monolith with explicit service boundaries

Status: Accepted for prototype; revisit after workflow and customer validation.

## Context
ATLAS needs a secure control plane, AI/data experimentation, a developer-facing client and a policy layer. The founder has limited hardware and no funded infrastructure.

## Decision
- Go owns the initial task API, concurrency, state transitions and append-only audit ledger.
- Python owns deterministic AI baselines, evaluation and future model integrations.
- TypeScript owns the SDK and operator experience.
- Rust provides a pure policy kernel reference for later integration.
- PostgreSQL is the intended durable multi-tenant datastore; the current Go demonstrator uses a local JSONL ledger and in-memory task projection.
- Domain boundaries are modular; no microservice split beyond the AI service until deployment, ownership or scale justifies it.

## Consequences
Positive: strong language/tool fit, low startup cost, clean boundaries, independently testable slices.
Negative: multiple toolchains, protocol drift risk, extra deployment complexity and duplicated validation until conformance tests exist.

## Rejected alternatives
- A single-language stack for every concern: rejected because AI and systems constraints differ materially.
- A microservice per subsystem: rejected as premature operational overhead.
- Custom kernel, foundation model or database: rejected absent measured requirements and resources.
''')

